**What we’re doing**  

We’ll add Google Analytics (GA4) so you can see how visitors use the site, but we’ll keep it *completely safe*: the tracking ID will live in a hidden environment variable and never appears in the public code. No personal data will be sent to GA.

---

## One‑screen plan

| Item | Details |
|------|---------|
| **What we are building** | A safe “GA4 tracking” snippet for a Next.js Next‑page |
| **Who uses it** | Your site visitors – anyone who comes to your UK business page |
| **Risk level** | **Green** – just a public analytics snippet, no logins, no personal data, no payments |
| **Day‑one features** | 1. Safely read GA tracking ID from env 2. Insert a `<script>` tag in `_app.js` 3. Verify the code runs in dev & prod |
| **Information it stores** | None – GA just receives public page load data |
| **Logins** | No logins needed |
| **Money** | No payments |
| **Built with** | Next.js (React) + Vercel (hosting) |
| **Steps** | 1. Add env variable `NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID` 2. Update `pages/_app.js` to load GA 3. Deploy & test |
| **Safety** | The only secret (GA measurement ID) is in an env var that Vercel hides from the browser (`NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix means it *is* visible, but it’s only a public ID, not a secret key). We’ll create a **save point** before any file changes. |
| **Not in version one** | No other tracking, no custom events, no analytics dashboards |

**Does this look right?** Reply `yes` or tell me what to tweak.

---

## Step 1 – Create an environment variable

1. In your project root create (or edit) a file called `[project]/.env.local`.
2. Add the line:  

   ```
   NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-XXXXXXXXXX
   ```

   *Replace `G-XXXXXXXXXX` with your actual GA‑measurement ID.*

3. **Save a point:** Commit this change or zip the folder (if you’re not using git).

> **What this does:** Vercel will inject `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID` into the client bundle, making it available to your JavaScript. Because it is prefixed with `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, it will appear in the browser – that’s intentional for GA – but it’s a public id, not a secret.

---

## Step 2 – Inject GA in `_app.js`

Open or create `pages/_app.js` (or `app/layout.tsx` if you’re using the new App router). Add the following at the top of the file:

```js
if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
  // Load GA only in the browser
  import('../lib/gtag').then(({ default: initGA }) => initGA());
}
```

Create a new file `lib/gtag.js` (or `lib/gtag.ts` if you use TypeScript) with:

```js
// lib/gtag.js
export const GA_MEASUREMENT_ID = process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID;

export const gtagConfig = {
  page_path: window.location.pathname,
};

export const pageview = (url) => {
  if (!GA_MEASUREMENT_ID) return; // safety guard
  window.gtag('config', GA_MEASUREMENT_ID, {
    ...gtagConfig,
    page_path: url,
  });
};

export const initGA = () => {
  if (!GA_MEASUREMENT_ID) {
    console.warn('GA_MEASUREMENT_ID not set');
    return;
  }

  /* Load gtag.js */
  const script = document.createElement('script');
  script.src = `https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=${GA_MEASUREMENT_ID}`;
  script.async = true;
  document.head.appendChild(script);

  /* Initialise gtag */
  window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
  function gtag(){window.dataLayer.push(arguments);}
  window.gtag = gtag;
  gtag('js', new Date());
  gtag('config', GA_MEASUREMENT_ID, { page_path: window.location.pathname });
};
```

**What this does**

- Adds a small script that loads GA’s global tag library (`gtag.js`).
- Calls `gtag('config', …)` when the page loads.
- Uses the measurement ID from the env variable.

> **Safety note:** The ID in the env file is only public data that GA expects; no secrets are exposed.

---

## Step 3 – Test locally

Run your dev server:

```bash
npm run dev   # or yarn dev
```

Open the console in the browser (F12 → Console). You should see a line like:

```
gtag.js loaded  (or nothing if you suppressed it)
```

Take a look at the Network tab; you should see a request to `https://www.google-analytics.com/collect`. That’s normal.

Switch to production (deploy to Vercel) and open the site. In the browser console again, you should see the same `gtag.js` request. In the GA dashboard, you’ll start seeing pageviews after a few minutes.

---

## Final touch – Commit & Deploy

1. Commit your changes (`.env.local` must stay **outside** of source control if you are using public Git; Vercel will manage it from its dashboard).  
   *If you are using a private repo, you can commit – just keep in mind it’s safe to include the measurement ID in Git.*

2. Push to Vercel.  
   The env variable can also be set in the Vercel dashboard under **Settings → Environment Variables** to keep it invisible in your repo.

---

**Next steps**

- If you want to track page views automatically when users navigate (client‑side routing), add the following in `pages/_app.js` after the `initGA();` line:

  ```js
  import { useRouter } from 'next/router';
  import { useEffect } from 'react';
  import { pageview } from '../lib/gtag';

  const MyApp = ({ Component, pageProps }) => {
    const router = useRouter();

    useEffect(() => {
      const handleRouteChange = (url) => {
        pageview(url);
      };
      router.events.on('routeChangeComplete', handleRouteChange);
      return () => {
        router.events.off('routeChangeComplete', handleRouteChange);
      };
    }, [router.events]);

    return <Component {...pageProps} />;
  };

  export default MyApp
